pseudo-Boolean proof version 2.0
* Regression test (wrong verdict): an order definition proved its transitivity
* before the `def` block that supplies the relation.
*
* `def_order`/`pre_order` is a SEQUENCE -- `vars`, an optional `spec`, `def`,
* an optional `transitivity`, an optional `reflexivity`, `end` -- and every
* side condition of the order is checked against the definition `def` supplies.
* `OrderProofRule` builds the transitivity obligations by iterating
* `constructed_order.definition` at the moment `proof` runs, one proofgoal per
* definition constraint. Written before `def`, that iteration is over an empty
* list, so the two lines `proof` / `end` below discharged transitivity for a
* relation that did not exist yet -- and the order's closing `end` then skips
* the real check, because `transitivity_proven` is already set.
*
* Only the version-3 grammar enforced the sequence: `Parser::def_order_rule`
* reads the blocks in that order and errors on anything else, while the
* version-2 parser dispatches every line through one flat match. Same shape as
* wrong verdict 23, one level down -- that one pinned WHICH rules may appear
* inside an order definition, this one pins WHEN.
*
* The round-4 freshness guard passes cleanly here: `fresh_right w1 w2 w3` has
* exactly `|right|` entries, all fresh and pairwise distinct. What is wrong is
* not the transitivity proof but that there was nothing to prove.
*
* The relation `def` then supplies orders x1 < x2 < x3 < x1, and one `dom`
* chases the cycle to the empty contradiction. The formula is
* exactly-one-of-three and is satisfied by `x1 = 1, x2 = 0, x3 = 0` (and by the
* two rotations); this used to print `s VERIFIED UNSATISFIABLE` at exit 0 under
* default flags, from the same binary that verifies
* correct/version2/pre_order_transitivity_after_def.
f 4
pre_order cyc
    vars
        left u1 u2 u3
        right v1 v2 v3
        aux
    end
    transitivity
        vars
            fresh_right w1 w2 w3
        end
        proof
        end
    end
    def
        1 ~u1 1 v1 1 v2 >= 1 ;
        1 ~u2 1 v2 1 v3 >= 1 ;
        1 ~u3 1 v3 1 v1 >= 1 ;
    end
end
load_order cyc x1 x2 x3
dom >= 1 ; x1 -> x2 x2 -> x3 x3 -> x1 ; begin
    proofgoal #4
        u 1 ~x1 >= 1 ;
        u 1 ~x2 >= 1 ;
        u 1 ~x3 >= 1 ;
        u >= 1 ;
    end
end
output NONE
conclusion UNSAT
end pseudo-Boolean proof
